  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 415 PM EDT TUE OCT 27 2026\n\nThe extended period begins Thursday morning with the region\npositioned beneath an anomalously strong upper-level ridge over\nthe Southeast CONUS. Over eastern Kentucky, 500-mb heights around\n582 dam lie within the 90th climatological percentile, \nhighlighting the unseasonable nature of this pattern. At the \nsurface, strong high pressure centered just east of the area will \nmaintain light south- to-southwesterly flow beneath mostly sunny \nto clear skies. Temperatures will peak in the low-to-mid 70s, \nrunning 5 to 10 degrees above late-October normals. With clear \nskies, dry air, and light winds in place, efficient nocturnal \nradiational cooling Thursday night will promote pronounced ridge- \nvalley temperature splits. Sheltered river valleys are expected to\ndecouple into the low-to-mid 40s - favoring patchy river valley \nfog development - while thermal belt ridges cool into the upper \n40s.\n\nHeading into the weekend (Friday through Sunday), upstream changes\nbegin taking shape across the CONUS. To our southwest, the remnants\nof Tropical Storm Vance - currently in the Eastern Pacific - will\nweaken and lift northeast over the higher terrain of Mexico before\ngetting ingested into the subtropical jet. Further upstream, broader\ntroughing over the western CONUS will feature an upper-level low\ndigging from the Pacific Northwest into the Great Basin. As this\nwestern trough amplifies into the Desert Southwest, it will tap that\ningested tropical moisture plume and channel it northeastward ahead\nof a deepening central Plains cyclone, setting the stage for surging\nmoisture values and an increasingly unsettled regime across the Ohio\nValley.\n\nCloser to home, Friday will remain dry and unseasonably warm with\nhighs once again reaching the mid-70s. A surface cold front will  \nstall over the area later Friday before the boundary lifts back \nnorth across the Ohio Valley as a warm front by Saturday morning. \nClouds will begin increasing Friday evening and persist across the\nregion through Saturday, though afternoon temperatures should \nstill manage the low-to-mid 70s before deeper moisture arrives. As\nthat deeper moisture plume advects in from the southwest, shower \nchances will ramp up late Saturday evening into the overnight \nhours, persisting through the day Sunday.\n\nConsiderable uncertainty remains regarding Sunday's high\ntemperatures due to a noticeable spread in ensemble guidance. The\nNBM ensemble spread favors a milder outcome with highs in the\nlow-to-mid 60s, whereas the 00Z ECMWF ensemble drops temperatures\ninto the 10th to 2.5th climatological percentile - suggesting highs\nstruggling to escape the 50s. This difference comes down to the\nprogression of rain into the area. A quicker arrival of showers\nwill lead to unseasonably cool conditions, while a slower arrival \nallows temperatures to warm into the 60s.\n\nMonday, energy from the western trough ejects eastward into the\nCentral Plains while the remnants of the weekend shortwave move off\nto the northeast. Scattered showers are expected through much of the\nday as low-level flow turns southerly, boosting highs into the lower\n70s. Tuesday, with mean PWATs around 1.1 to 1.2 inches (exceeding\nthe 90th climatological percentile), shower chances will ramp up\nthrough the afternoon into the evening as the cold front and parent\ntrough axis push into the Ohio Valley, including eastern Kentucky.\nTemperatures should remain in the lower 70s ahead of the boundary\nunder breezy south-to-southeasterly flow.",
